Database migrations
===================
Run commands from the repository root. Both app/database.py and Alembic load
.env without overriding process environment variables. DATABASE_URL defaults
to sqlite+aiosqlite:///./bot.db. PostgreSQL uses postgresql+asyncpg://...
Never print credentials or replace the existing .env during migration work.

Baseline revision: b9a1e47cb84b. Current single HEAD: a8b4d6e21c90.
Production databases previously at b9a1e47cb84b must run `alembic upgrade head`
before deploying code that requires the Stage 17A/18A columns. Do not use stamp
to skip this forward migration.

Fresh EMPTY database only:
  py -m alembic upgrade head
This creates the five application tables, indexes, foreign keys and version.
Application startup still uses create_all and retains the old additive SQLite
column patches for legacy MVP databases. Startup does not stamp or run Alembic.
For production, migrate explicitly before starting application workers.

Existing populated, UNSTAMPED SQLite database:
1. Open in read-only mode; audit schema, indexes, counts, identifiers, duplicate
   keys, orphan references, integrity_check and foreign_key_check.
2. Verify its schema matches the baseline. stamp does NOT validate schema.
3. Use sqlite3.Connection.backup to a NEW timestamped file. A raw copy of a
   running WAL database can omit committed WAL data. Verify the backup exists,
   opens cleanly, and has matching application contents.
4. If the existing populated SQLite schema is truly the old baseline schema,
   stamp the BASELINE first (not HEAD), then apply reviewed forward migrations:
     py -m alembic stamp b9a1e47cb84b
     py -m alembic upgrade head
5. Compare complete application rows, identifiers and schema before/after.
   Only alembic_version and its index should be added. Account for independent
   live writes if any; never undo them merely to force an audit comparison.
Do not apply the baseline upgrade to existing unstamped tables. Never run
baseline downgrade on a populated database: it drops application tables.

Future changes: generate/review migrations against a disposable database,
test upgrade and restore paths there, back up production, then apply the
reviewed forward revision in a controlled maintenance workflow. SQLite batch
alterations may rebuild tables; they require explicit review. Keep legacy
SQLite patches until old database compatibility has been independently proven.

Validation: py -m pytest -q uses temporary SQLite files even if the parent
process has DATABASE_URL set. Baseline schema comparison, metadata-only stamping,
percent-containing URLs and PostgreSQL offline DDL are tested. Offline SQL is
not a substitute for integration tests on a real PostgreSQL server.
